doctrine.md is that repository's axiom_agent/DOCTRINE.md, unchanged; its
README dedicates both copies to the public domain under CC0 1.0. What was
rewritten: the standing injection is a harness section (`standing-section`
advising `nle::read-harness-sections`) instead of a pre-model-call hook, and
the two forms and their caps are kept -- 1,800 characters compact, 4,000 full,
800 for the owner's additions -- with the compact form built from each
article's opening sentence where the original selected sentences by position,
so the shipped wording stays the only copy of the prose. The `/doctrine`
command keeps its four verbs (status, show, show n, plugins) and its footer,
and the same answers are Lisp verbs in the `doctrine` package. The owner's
additions file is kept, read with the image's own reader, from the home's
`doctrine.md` or a path the section names, with the same 800-character cap in
a request and no cap in `show`.

What was left out: the Hermes runtime probes. The original asked its host
`ctx.has_plugin()` for five enforcer plugins and scanned the top-level `name:`
line of their `plugin.yaml` manifests under the Hermes user-plugins folder, to
say an article was ENFORCED. Those plugins are separate cells here, so the
doctrine's own `*Enforced by:*` lines are parsed as the original parsed them
and each name is mapped to the cell that carries it; a cell is installed when
a folder of that name sits in the home's cells folder, where presence is
enabled. There is no manifest scan, no YAML of any kind, and no probe of a
running plugin, so `standing` and `hermes-gatekeeper` read ADVISED until a cell
carries them. The `form`/`inject` settings are the original's; `additions` and
`cells_dir` are added so both the owner's file and the report can be pointed
somewhere in a test.
